// @@@ If the index is between 0 and the initial size of the list inclusive, then for every valid index up to the specified index, each element is either null and was null before, or remains unchanged from its previous state.
```

We need to produce an assert statement reflecting that.

Context: method add(int index, Object element) overrides ArrayList.add. It inserts element at index, shifting subsequent elements right. The comment says: If index is between 0 and the initial size inclusive, then for every valid index up to the specified index, each element is either null and was null before, or remains unchanged from its previous state.

Interpretation: initial size = \old(this.size()). The index is between 0 and initial size inclusive (i.e., 0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())).

Then for every valid index up to the specified index (i.e., for all i such that 0 <= i && i <= index), each element is either null and was null before, or remains unchanged from its previous state.

But after insertion, elements shift. For indices less than index, elements should stay same as before. At index, the new element is inserted, could be null or something. The comment says each element is either null and was null before, or remains unchanged. That suggests for each i up to index, after operation, either this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null, or this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))) (or same reference). But need to express unchanged. Since elements are Objects, we can use == or equals? In examples they used .equals for query. For generic object list, maybe use ==. Use \old(this.get(i)).

But we also need to consider that for i == index, element is the inserted one; the condition "either null and was null before, or remains unchanged" seems to allow that if inserted element is null and previously there was null at that position (but before insertion, there was no element at that index). Actually before insertion, index may be equal to size, meaning inserting at end. For i == index, previous state had no element (out of bounds). The statement may treat that as "null and was null before". But they treat get(i) maybe returns null for out-of-bounds? Not defined. Might just apply to indices up to index-1. But comment says "up to the specified index". We'll follow comment.

Thus assert:

assert (0 <= index && index <= \old(this.size())) => 
       (\forall int i; 0 <= i && i <= index;
           (this.get(i) == null && \old(this.get(i)) == null) || this.get(i).equals(\old(this.get(i))));

Need to use .equals for non-null. But if both null, equals would throw NPE. So we separate null case.

Thus final assert.

